        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A shank is the backbone for articulated streamers and steelhead Intruders. The trailing hook holds the fish. The shank builds length and movement.</p><p>Shorter shanks keep Intruders compact. Longer shanks stretch baitfish and multi-section streamers. Wire should carry the dressing without folding once the fly is wet. Match shank length to the forage you are imitating, then pick a trailing hook that fits the game fish.</p><h3>Articulated vs single-hook builds</h3><p>Use shanks when the fly needs a joint and a longer profile. Keep the trailing hook honest to the water you fish. If the pattern is a simple Intruder-style build, start with the shank length you already fish, not the longest tube on the wall.</p><p>Dress the shank with <a href=\"/collections/fly-tying-materials\">Fly Tying Materials</a>, <a href=\"/collections/tails-fins-claws-more\">Tails, Fins, Claws &amp; More</a>, and <a href=\"/collections/feathers\">Feathers</a>.</p>"
          }
